From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 03:44:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] clutter/color: Anchor the Night Light adaptation at the
 default white

The diagonal white adaptation scaled the output's encoding channels by the
linear RGB of the absolute Planckian white. At the default temperature
there is no adaptation at all, bit for bit, but the Planckian white of
6500 K is not D65 (du'v' about 0.004), so 1 K below it the factors were
already about (0.993, 0.958, 1): a step of about 4 % in green at every
Night Light onset and end, and on an output with a DCI white a jump of
about 0.017 du'v'.

Make the factors relative to a reference white the display target
carries alongside the adapted white: each is the ratio of the white's
channel to the reference's, in linear RGB of the encoding, clamped at
zero and divided by the largest. The color device passes the Planckian
white of the default temperature, computed by the same function as the
temperature's, so the adaptation is exactly identity at the reference,
continuous where it starts, and moves the output's own white by the same
per-channel ratio as the gamma ramp of Night Light in SDR. A reference
white outside the encoding's gamut is refused. The reference is part of
the state's equality, identity, hashes and description. The exact
default-temperature bypass is kept.

Representable whites are now exact relative to the default temperature's
Planckian white, not absolute: an output's white keeps its own offset
from that white. The 1000 K white is still projected. Update the factor,
white and representable regressions to an independent double precision
computation of the ratio, check the reference white through the color
device, and add continuity regressions at 6499 K and 6501 K on BT.2020
and DCI-white outputs.

This follows the review decision to anchor at the default temperature
rather than to adapt at 6500 K or to use the daylight locus.
